    "summary": "Dark Star shines; quirky Cryptical→Caution flow adds charm",
    "blurb": "The set delivers a powerful Dark Star and an adventurous Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One → Caution sequence. Phil Lesh’s insistence on the unusual segue gives the show a distinctive, off‑beat pacing. While Saint Stephen and The Eleven feel a bit tentative, the overall performance is rewarding for completists.",
    "review": "April 17 1969 at Washington University’s Quadrangle captures the Dead in an experimental mood. A solid soundboard source lets you hear Jerry’s soaring leads on Dark Star and the band’s willingness to stretch out into the cryptic Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One → Caution suite, a segue that Phil Lesh championed that day. The early blues numbers—Hard To Handle and Morning Dew—set a loose tone, but the real intrigue lies in the middle of the set where the group flirts with chaos: Saint Stephen’s fragmented reprise and a tentative Eleven feel like a rehearsal stumble, yet they underscore the night’s daring spirit. Pigpen and Tom provide their usual textural layers, while Mickey and Bill keep the rhythmic engine steady despite a few sound‑engineer hiccups noted in the mix. For fans of the late‑’60s exploratory period, this recording offers a rare glimpse into a show that balances tight jams with experimental detours.",
